{parent=Pharmacology}

= Diuretic
{parent=Pharmacology}

A medicine that increases urinary excretion of water and usually particular dissolved substances. The indication, fluid balance and monitoring requirements depend on the cause of the fluid retention.

= Antimicrobial resistance
{parent=Pharmacology}

Reduced susceptibility of a microorganism to an antimicrobial agent, requiring attention to organism identification, susceptibility and the clinical syndrome.

= Ferri et Quiniae Citras
{parent=Pharmacology}
{c}

Historical pharmaceutical preparation; its composition and clinical use must be identified by edition. <The Essentials of Materia Medica and Therapeutics by Alfred Baring Garrod (1865)>[corresponding monograph and British preparation]{ref}

= Atropine
{parent=Pharmacology}

= Unguentum Atropiae
{parent=Atropine}
{c}

Historical pharmaceutical preparation; its composition and clinical use must be identified by edition. <The Essentials of Materia Medica and Therapeutics by Alfred Baring Garrod (1865)>[corresponding monograph and British preparation]{ref}

= Liquor Atropiae
{parent=Atropine}
{c}

Historical pharmaceutical preparation; its composition and clinical use must be identified by edition. <The Essentials of Materia Medica and Therapeutics by Alfred Baring Garrod (1865)>[corresponding monograph and British preparation]{ref}

= Copper sulfate
{parent=Pharmacology}

Copper-containing salt used historically for astringent, emetic and external applications; period use does not establish current safety.

= Ferrous arsenate
{parent=Pharmacology}

An arsenic-containing historical medicinal article, Ferri arsenias.

= Sodium arsenate
{parent=Pharmacology}

An arsenic-containing salt appearing in historical materia medica; hydrated and dried forms have different mass composition.

= Liquor Sodae Arseniatis
{parent=Sodium arsenate}
{c}

Historical pharmaceutical preparation; its composition and clinical use must be identified by edition. <The Essentials of Materia Medica and Therapeutics by Alfred Baring Garrod (1865)>[corresponding monograph and British preparation]{ref}

= Arsenical solution
{parent=Pharmacology}

Historical solution of arsenite and carbonate of potassium, specified as Liquor arsenicalis in the 1864 British standard. It is not a modern general-purpose tonic.

= Turpentine ointment
{parent=Pharmacology}

Historical external preparation containing <turpentine>.

= Soap liniment
{parent=Pharmacology}

Historical soap-containing external preparation, also containing camphor in the British formula.

= Camphor liniment
{parent=Pharmacology}

Historical camphor-containing external preparation. Simple and compound forms differ in composition.

= Linimentum Camphorae Compositum
{parent=Camphor liniment}
{c}

Historical pharmaceutical preparation; its composition and clinical use must be identified by edition. <The Essentials of Materia Medica and Therapeutics by Alfred Baring Garrod (1865)>[corresponding monograph and British preparation]{ref}

= Ammonia liniment
{parent=Pharmacology}

Historical external preparation containing <ammonia>; its presence on a skin surface is not evidence of benefit to a distant organ.

= Linimentum Ammoniae
{parent=Ammonia liniment}
{c}

Historical pharmaceutical preparation; its composition and clinical use must be identified by edition. <The Essentials of Materia Medica and Therapeutics by Alfred Baring Garrod (1865)>[corresponding monograph and British preparation]{ref}

= Tannic acid lozenge
{parent=Pharmacology}

A historical astringent lozenge containing <tannic acid>. Edition-specific formulation and the number administered must be distinguished.

= Salicylate
{parent=Pharmacology}

= Anti-inflammatory drug
{parent=Pharmacology}

A drug intended to reduce inflammation; the indication and benefit depend on the underlying disorder.

