= Vesicant
{parent=Pharmacology}

An agent capable of producing a <blister>. A <vesicant> effect is evidence of local injury, not by itself evidence of therapeutic benefit.

= Cantharidin
{parent=Vesicant}

A toxic natural <vesicant> obtained from <blister beetles>. Its topical action can cause <acantholysis> and <blister> formation.<FDA multidisciplinary review of YCANTH>[NDA 212905, §5.3, p. 32]{ref}

= Laxative
{parent=Pharmacology}

An agent that promotes defecation. Some act predominantly within the intestinal lumen rather than through systemic <drug absorption>.

= Purgative
{parent=Laxative}

A medicine producing bowel evacuation, historically often implying a more vigorous effect than a mild <laxative>.

= Polyethylene glycol 3350
{parent=Laxative}

A polymeric osmotic <laxative>; 3350 denotes its approximate average molecular mass. Retention of <water> in the intestinal lumen accounts for its local action.<Polyethylene Glycol 3350 by MedlinePlus>[Why is this medication prescribed?]{ref}

= Pharmacological effect
{parent=Pharmacology}

A change in biological function caused by a <drug>. Direct action at a target should be distinguished from a secondary <physiological response>.

= Drug
{parent=Pharmacology}

A substance used to modify biological function for a therapeutic, diagnostic or experimental purpose.

= Antibiotic prophylaxis
{parent=Pharmacology}

= Secondary antibiotic prophylaxis
{parent=Antibiotic prophylaxis}

Antibiotic treatment intended to prevent recurrence after a previous disease episode, including recurrence of <acute rheumatic fever>.

= Uterotonic
{parent=Pharmacology}

= Corticosteroid
{parent=Pharmacology}

= Pharmacopoeia
{parent=Pharmacology}

An official standard specifying the identity, preparation and quality of medicinal substances. An edition must be specified when interpreting historical prescriptions.

= British Pharmacopoeia
{c}
{parent=Pharmacopoeia}

= Pharmacognosy
{parent=Pharmacology}

Study and identification of medicinal materials obtained from natural sources, including the particular <plant> organ and its preparation.

= Cantharides
{parent=Pharmacognosy}

Historical dried blister-beetle medicinal material containing <cantharidin>. Its toxicity differs from the efficacy question for a particular regulated topical product.

= Liquor Epispasticus
{parent=Cantharides}
{c}

Historical pharmaceutical preparation; its composition and clinical use must be identified by edition. <The Essentials of Materia Medica and Therapeutics by Alfred Baring Garrod (1865)>[corresponding monograph and British preparation]{ref}

= Unguentum Cantharidis
{parent=Cantharides}
{c}

