= Capsicum
{parent=Plant medicinal material}

Historical medicinal capsicum fruit material, distinct from a dose of an isolated modern drug.

= Mustard
{parent=Plant medicinal material}

Mustard seed materials used in historical preparations. Moistening crushed black-mustard <seeds> generates an irritant volatile product containing <allyl isothiocyanate>; the volatile product differs from the fixed seed <oil>.

= Cataplasma Sinapis
{parent=Mustard}
{c}

Historical pharmaceutical preparation; its composition and clinical use must be identified by edition. <The Essentials of Materia Medica and Therapeutics by Alfred Baring Garrod (1865)>[corresponding monograph and British preparation]{ref}

= Kino
{parent=Plant medicinal material}

A dried plant exudate used historically as an astringent; it must not be confused with the <heartwood> specified as red sandalwood.

= Croton oil
{parent=Plant medicinal material}

The <oil> expressed from the <seeds> of <Croton tiglium>; a strongly irritant historical medicinal material.

= Linimentum Crotonis
{parent=Croton oil}
{c}

Historical pharmaceutical preparation; its composition and clinical use must be identified by edition. <The Essentials of Materia Medica and Therapeutics by Alfred Baring Garrod (1865)>[corresponding monograph and British preparation]{ref}

= Cocoa butter
{parent=Plant medicinal material}

The concrete <fat> obtained from the ground <seeds> of <Theobroma cacao> by expression and heat; Oleum theobromæ in the 1867 standard.

= Squill
{parent=Plant medicinal material}

The sliced and dried <bulb> specified as Scilla in the <British Pharmacopoeia by the General Medical Council (1867)>, botanically attributed there to Urginea scilla, with Scilla maritima as another name. The medicinal organ is a <bulb>, not a <root (botany)>.

= Syrupus Scillae
{parent=Squill}
{c}

Historical formulation name; composition is identified by pharmacopoeial edition.

= Tinctura Scillae
{parent=Squill}
{c}

Historical formulation name; composition is identified by pharmacopoeial edition.

= Pilula Scillae Composita
{parent=Squill}
{c}

Historical formulation name; composition is identified by pharmacopoeial edition.

= Calabar bean
{parent=Plant medicinal material}
{c}

The medicinal <seed> of <Physostigma venenosum>; the 1867 article is Physostigmatis faba. Its apparently ordinary seed characters do not imply that it is edible.

= Cinchona bark
{parent=Plant medicinal material}

Medicinal <bark> obtained from specified species of Cinchona; a historical source of <quinine> and related <cinchona alkaloids>.

= Tinctura Cinchonae Composita
{parent=Cinchona bark}
{c}

Historical pharmaceutical preparation; its composition and clinical use must be identified by edition. <The Essentials of Materia Medica and Therapeutics by Alfred Baring Garrod (1865)>[corresponding monograph and British preparation]{ref}

= Tinctura Cinchonae Flavae
{parent=Cinchona bark}
{c}

Historical pharmaceutical preparation; its composition and clinical use must be identified by edition. <The Essentials of Materia Medica and Therapeutics by Alfred Baring Garrod (1865)>[corresponding monograph and British preparation]{ref}

= Extractum Cinchonae Flavae Liquidum
{parent=Cinchona bark}
{c}

Historical pharmaceutical preparation; its composition and clinical use must be identified by edition. <The Essentials of Materia Medica and Therapeutics by Alfred Baring Garrod (1865)>[corresponding monograph and British preparation]{ref}

= Infusum Cinchonae Flavae
{parent=Cinchona bark}
{c}

Historical pharmaceutical preparation; its composition and clinical use must be identified by edition. <The Essentials of Materia Medica and Therapeutics by Alfred Baring Garrod (1865)>[corresponding monograph and British preparation]{ref}

= Decoctum Cinchonae Flavae
{parent=Cinchona bark}
{c}

Historical pharmaceutical preparation; its composition and clinical use must be identified by edition. <The Essentials of Materia Medica and Therapeutics by Alfred Baring Garrod (1865)>[corresponding monograph and British preparation]{ref}

= Adverse drug reaction
{parent=Pharmacology}

A harmful or unintended response attributed to a medicine under specified conditions. Temporal association alone does not establish drug causation; disease, concomitant <treatments> and contextual influences must also be assessed.

= Ketamine
{parent=Pharmacology}

A compound with NMDA-receptor <antagonist> actions among other effects, used in some experiments to perturb <learning> and perception. A transient pharmacological model does not reproduce an entire psychiatric disorder.

= D-cycloserine
{parent=Pharmacology}
{c}

A compound studied as a partial <agonist> at the glycine site of the <NMDA receptor> and as a possible facilitator of <learning>. An experimental adjunct to a <learning> session is not equivalent to a general <anxiety> suppressant.

= Antipsychotic
{parent=Pharmacology}

A medicine used for psychotic symptoms. <Receptor> effects and symptom improvement do not by themselves explain a patient’s original disease cause or erase learned beliefs.

= Antagonist
{parent=Pharmacology}

A ligand that reduces <receptor> activation by an <agonist> or endogenous signal under specified conditions. Behavioural selectivity cannot be inferred solely from an in-vitro affinity label.

= Benzodiazepine
{parent=Pharmacology}

A drug class including positive allosteric modulators at susceptible <GABA-A receptors>. A behavioural effect can involve anxiolysis, sedation, altered <memory in animals>[memory] or movement, which must be distinguished.

= Neuropsychopharmacology
{parent=Pharmacology}

Study of how pharmacological manipulations interact with <neural circuits> and psychological functions. A change in task performance can arise through altered <learning>, retrieval, <motivation>, sensation or motor execution; the mechanism must be distinguished experimentally.

= Psychopharmacology
{parent=Pharmacology}

Study of how drugs affect psychological and behavioral functioning and of their clinical use. Treatment response to a drug does not alone identify a disorder's original cause.

= Risperidone
{parent=Psychopharmacology}

= Imipramine
{parent=Psychopharmacology}

= Clomipramine
{parent=Psychopharmacology}

A tricyclic antidepressant with serotonin-reuptake-inhibiting action, studied in <obsessive-compulsive disorder>. Comparative efficacy must be interpreted alongside tolerability and the treatment schedule; results for it do not automatically describe every <SSRI>.

= Antidepressant
{parent=Pharmacology}

A medicine used to treat depressive illness. A demonstrated treatment effect does not, by itself, identify the biochemical abnormality that originally caused the illness.

= Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor
{parent=Antidepressant}

= SSRI
{c}
{synonym}

An antidepressant class whose principal initial pharmacological action inhibits serotonin transport back into presynaptic cells. Initial transporter inhibition and subsequent clinical improvement need not have the same time course.

= Fluoxetine
{parent=Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor}

A <selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor> studied in several <mental disorders>. A favorable treatment response does not by itself establish that a simple serotonin deficiency caused the condition.<A randomized controlled trial of medication and cognitive-behavioral therapy for hypochondriasis by Brian A. Fallon and others>[2017; randomized pharmacotherapy comparison and qualified efficacy outcomes]{ref}

= Off-target drug effect
{parent=Pharmacology}

A drug effect mediated through an interaction other than the intended target. A residual phenotype after verified protection of the intended target is a useful candidate, but requires independent causal validation.

= Drug hypersensitivity
{parent=Pharmacology}

An increased response to a drug relative to a specified control. Cellular hypersensitivity does not uniquely imply higher molecular affinity for the intended target.

= Disc diffusion assay
{parent=Pharmacology}

= Halo assay
{synonym}

A growth-inhibition assay in which a compound diffuses from a disc into a lawn of <biological cells>. Halo size combines susceptibility with <molecular diffusion> and growth conditions and is not directly a molecular <binding affinity>.

= Pharmacokinetics
{parent=Pharmacology}

= Drug absorption
{parent=Pharmacokinetics}

Transfer of a <drug> from its administration site into the systemic circulation. Direct intravenous delivery bypasses this transfer step.

= Target engagement
{parent=Pharmacology}

Evidence that an intervention interacts with its intended molecular target in the biological setting being tested. A downstream phenotype alone is insufficient.

= Therapeutic window
{parent=Pharmacology}

A range of exposures giving useful benefit while keeping adverse effects acceptable. Biochemical target inhibition alone does not establish this range.

= Ipilimumab
{parent=Pharmacology}

A therapeutic antibody directed against <CTLA-4>, used as an immune checkpoint inhibitor in defined clinical contexts.

= Chemoresistance
{parent=Pharmacology}

Reduced susceptibility of cells or disease to a chemotherapy regimen. Survival can reflect pre-existing resistant states, induced changes or genetic evolution.

= Ulixertinib
{parent=Pharmacology}

A small-molecule inhibitor of <ERK> used experimentally to test pathway requirements. Developmental interpretation requires stage, dose, target-engagement and viability controls.

= Pharmacologist
{parent=Pharmacology}

